  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1245 PM CDT Thu Oct 1 2026\n\nWV imagery indicates a closed upper low transitioning east through \nthe high plains of western Kansas and the panhandles of Oklahoma and \nTexas. Near the surface, a stalled frontal boundary attendant to a \nlow in extreme southwest Kansas extends northeast into southeast \nNebraska. \n\nBased on radar trends/CAMs, ongoing showers/thunderstorms will continue\nto develop southward across much of southwest Kansas this afternoon,\nthen end west to east later this evening as the SREF indicates a closed\nupper low opening up as it begins to accelerate eastward through the\nCentral Plains tonight, giving way to a more zonal flow aloft across\nthe Western High Plains Friday. The main focus the next several hours\nwill be for possible flash flooding as already saturated ground from\nprolonged rainfall the previous day lends to a heightened flooding\npotential through early evening. This is supported with most models\nshowing high QPF signals as well as SPC Mesoanalysis indicating PW\nvalues nearing 1.5 inches. A much drier pattern is forecast to take\nhold across the region through the weekend as a developing upper level\nridge begins to amplify across the Intermountain West while much drier\nair in the lower/mid-levels dominates the Western High Plains. \n\nThe coolest temperatures of the season are likely tonight as cooler \nair begins to spill into western Kansas behind an advancing cold front,\ndropping H85 temperatures below 10C across much of southwest Kansas\nby daybreak Friday. According to the latest HREF, there is a better\nthan 90% probability of temperatures slipping below 50F in west central\nKansas into central Kansas in vicinity of the I-70 corridor to a 50-70%\nprobability of temperatures dropping below 55F in south central Kansas.\nSo, look for lows down into the 40s(F) northwest to the lower 50s(F)\nsoutheast. For a change, unseasonably cool temperatures are forecast\nFriday as departing surface high pressure through the Upper Midwest\nreinforces a cooler air mass in place across the high plains of western\nKansas within a northeastly upslope flow, holding H85 temperatures\ndown around 10-12C. With the HREF painting a widespread 90% probability\nof temperatures exceeding 60F, expect afternoon highs up into the\n60s(F) with the warmest highs in south central Kansas where there\nis a 80-90% probability of temperatures topping 65F. Rebounding\ntemperatures are to be expected Saturday as surface high pressure\ncontinues to push farther off to the east through the Great Lakes\nwhile lee side troughing redevelops in eastern Colorado, returning\nsoutherlies to western Kansas. This will tap into slightly warmer\nair to our south, helping push H85 temperatures above 13-15C. Under\nample sunshine, expect more seasonable temperatures with fairly\nwidespread 70s(F) for highs.",
